# __APP_NAME__ — server

Authentication, roles and permissions, and a profile, already working.

## Run it

```bash
npm install
cp .env.example .env.development.local   # set MONGO_URI and JWT_SECRET
npm test                                  # the RBAC grid
npm run dev
```

## What is here

| | |
|---|---|
| `POST /api/auth/signup` · `/login` | public, returns a token |
| `GET·PATCH /api/profile` | your own account |
| `PUT·DELETE /api/profile/avatar` | your picture |
| `POST /api/profile/password` | change your own password |
| `GET /api/health` | public |

## What to edit first

**`src/config/permissions.ts`** — your catalog. Everything reads from it: boot
syncs it into the database, `authorize()` checks against it, and the test grid
generates a case per key. Adding a feature means adding its four keys and
nothing else.

Then mount a router in `src/index.ts`, gate it with `authorize("thing:read")`,
and add each gated route to `src/tests/rbac/route-permission-manifest.ts`.

## Where the rest lives

The rules — token verification, permission matching, password and avatar
policy — come from the Gambit packages as pinned dependencies. What is in this
repo is the GLUE: which model roles live in, what your token carries, what your
permissions are.

That split is deliberate. Copied code drifts and cannot be fixed centrally;
linked code cannot be adapted. The line is: does every app need this to be the
same, or does every app need it to be its own?

## A fresh database has no roles

The first account signs in able to do nothing, and boot says so. Create a role
and grant it `roles:*` and `users:*` so somebody can administer the app. New
accounts get no role at all — nothing is granted until it is granted
deliberately.
